from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "6. First Corinthians 13: Love, and What Fails, Ceases and Vanishes"),
("p", "Claim: the thirteenth chapter sets the gifts beside charity and reports what Paul says becomes of three of them. The chapter is read in order, and the verse on which most arguments about duration turn, 13:10, is reported as it is written. The volume holds that the ruling on tongues and prophecy does not rest on identifying “that which is perfect.”"),
("sub", "6.1 The gifts without charity"),
("p", "The chapter opens with three cases of a gift without love. “Though I speak with the tongues of men and of angels, and have not charity, I am become as sounding brass, or a tinkling cymbal” (1 Corinthians 13:1). “And though I have the gift of prophecy, and understand all mysteries, and all knowledge; and though I have all faith, so that I could remove mountains, and have not charity, I am nothing” (1 Corinthians 13:2). The third case concerns giving, even to the point of death: “it profiteth me nothing” (1 Corinthians 13:3)."),
("p", "The verse names “the tongues of men and of angels.” The phrase is joined to the conditional “Though I speak,” and it belongs with the other cases of the chapter, which are all stated as suppositions: “though I have all faith,” “though I bestow all my goods.” Paul does not say that any member of the church speaks with the tongues of angels. He says that if he did, and had not charity, he would be sounding brass. The volume reads 13:1 as a supposition of the same kind as 13:2, and it reads the phrase together with 14:10, 11, where tongues are compared to the kinds of voices in the world."),
("sub", "6.2 What charity does"),
("p", "Paul then describes charity in a run of short clauses, positive and negative: “Charity suffereth long, and is kind; charity envieth not; charity vaunteth not itself, is not puffed up” (1 Corinthians 13:4). The list concerns the conduct of members to one another, and it answers the disorder of the Corinthian assembly. It closes: “Beareth all things, believeth all things, hopeth all things, endureth all things” (1 Corinthians 13:7)."),
("sub", "6.3 Verse 8: three verbs, three gifts"),
("p", "Then the sentence on which the question turns: “Charity never faileth: but whether there be prophecies, they shall fail; whether there be tongues, they shall cease; whether there be knowledge, it shall vanish away” (1 Corinthians 13:8). Three gifts are named, prophecies, tongues and knowledge, and three verbs are used: fail, cease, vanish away. Charity is set against all three by the verb that Paul uses of it: it never faileth. The sentence says what will become of the gifts, and it says nothing about when."),
("p", "The verbs are not all the same in the Greek. The word the KJV renders “fail” of prophecies and “vanish away” of knowledge is one word, and the word it renders “cease” of tongues is another. Writers on both sides have used the difference to argue that tongues cease of themselves, before the others. The volume reports the difference and does not rest anything on it: the sentence affirms the end of all three and does not give an order."),
("sub", "6.4 Verses 9 to 12: in part, perfect, face to face"),
("p", "Paul continues: “For we know in part, and we prophesy in part” (1 Corinthians 13:9). The words place prophecy with knowledge: both are partial. He then gives the sentence that has been argued most: “But when that which is perfect is come, then that which is in part shall be done away” (1 Corinthians 13:10). Then two comparisons. “When I was a child, I spake as a child, I understood as a child, I thought as a child: but when I became a man, I put away childish things” (1 Corinthians 13:11). “For now we see through a glass, darkly; but then face to face: now I know in part; but then shall I know even as also I am known” (1 Corinthians 13:12)."),
("p", "The verse says that when that which is perfect has come, that which is in part will be done away. It does not say what the perfect is. The phrase has been read as the return of Christ, as the completed canon, as the maturity of the church, as the new heaven and earth, and as love itself. The volume names these readings and takes none. It does so for a reason stated in the thesis: the ruling on tongues does not rest on 13:10. It rests on 14:21, 22, on Isaiah 28 and on Acts 28. A reader who holds that the perfect is the return of Christ can still follow the argument, because the argument concerns the sign-program that the text assigns to the tongues, and not the date of the perfect. The rule of a plain report is kept: the verse is reported as written, the identification is left open, and no timing is drawn from the phrase."),
("p", "A reply to the writers who read 13:10 as the return of Christ appears in chapter 13. It is enough here to say that the verse sets a limit on the gifts of 13:8 and does not state a date, and that the volume gives the limit its place without supplying the date."),
("sub", "6.5 The readings of the perfect"),
("p", "The readings of 13:10 are many, and the volume sets them side by side so that the reader can see what each does to the ruling. The sentence is in the future: when that which is perfect is come, that which is in part shall be done away. The readings differ on what the perfect is."),
("ctbl", "The readings of that which is perfect.", [
    ("Reading", "What it makes of the verse", "Effect on the ruling"),
    ("The return of Christ", "The gifts stand until He comes", "None: the sign-program ended at Acts 28, and the verse names a final limit"),
    ("The completed Scriptures", "The gifts ended when revelation was complete", "None: the ruling rests on 14:21, 22 and Acts 28, not on the date of the Scriptures"),
    ("The maturity of the church", "The gifts give way as the church grows", "None: the sentence still sets a limit, and the ruling does not depend on a date"),
    ("The new heaven and earth", "The gifts stand to the end of the age", "None: the sign-program is not carried past Acts 28 by the verse"),
    ("Love itself", "Love is the perfect that outlasts the gifts", "None: the gifts still fail, cease and vanish"),
  ], [0.28, 0.34, 0.38]),
("p", "The table shows why the volume leaves the verse as it is written. Each reading gives a different answer to what the perfect is, and none changes where the sign-program stands. The identification would be a conclusion that the text itself does not draw. The volume does not draw it either."),
("sub", "6.6 Verse 13"),
("p", "The chapter ends: “And now abideth faith, hope, charity, these three; but the greatest of these is charity” (1 Corinthians 13:13). The word “now” is Paul’s present; the three that abide are set against the gifts of 13:8, which will fail, cease and vanish. The chapter thereby holds that the gifts of the Spirit are of a lesser order than the graces that remain, and the order stands whatever view is taken of the timing."),
("tbl", "First Corinthians 13:8–13 in order.", [
    ("1 Corinthians 13:8", T("1 Cor 13:8"), "Three gifts, three verbs, and no date."),
    ("1 Corinthians 13:9, 10", T("1 Cor 13:9, 10"), "Prophecy and knowledge are in part; that which is in part is done away when that which is perfect has come."),
    ("1 Corinthians 13:12", T("1 Cor 13:12"), "The contrast of now and then; the then is not named."),
    ("1 Corinthians 13:13", T("1 Cor 13:13"), "Faith, hope and charity abide."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "13:1 is a supposition of the same kind as 13:2 and 13:3, read with 14:10, 11.",
    "13:8 says prophecies shall fail, tongues shall cease and knowledge shall vanish away, and gives no date.",
    "13:10 is reported as written. The perfect is not identified, and the ruling does not depend on it.",
    "The chapter ranks the gifts below the graces that abide.",
  ]),
]
